WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:07.556
Where should AI apps run? On a laptop, a CPU server, GPUs, or the next generation of hardware?

2
00:00:07.936 --> 00:00:13.248
AI App Server is designed to run on all of them, and to size itself to the machine it finds.

3
00:00:13.628 --> 00:00:18.727
In this video, we'll be clear about what's tested on real machines, and what's still in preview.

4
00:00:19.807 --> 00:00:23.348
The first two items on this slide run on real machines today.

5
00:00:23.728 --> 00:00:28.187
The three marked preview are built, and tested on emulated hardware so far.

6
00:00:29.267 --> 00:00:40.104
At startup, the server reads the machine: CPU cores and instruction sets, memory and NUMA nodes, CXL memory, disks, GPUs, and data processors.

7
00:00:40.484 --> 00:00:45.647
aiapp hardware shows what it found. This is a real run, on an eight-core laptop.

8
00:00:46.027 --> 00:00:54.176
It also reports that this isn't a confidential VM, and that no data processor was found, so the CPUs do the data path.

9
00:00:55.256 --> 00:00:57.069
From that, it sizes itself.

10
00:00:57.449 --> 00:01:02.911
The key-value cache takes a sixteenth of memory, between sixty-four megabytes and eight gigabytes.

11
00:01:03.291 --> 00:01:10.907
Vectors stay full precision while they fit in a quarter of memory, and switch to eight-bit above that, with scores within about one hundredth.

12
00:01:11.287 --> 00:01:15.212
Local model threads follow the physical cores and the NUMA layout.

13
00:01:15.592 --> 00:01:24.637
And each kind of data has a preferred list of tiers, from GPU memory down to NVMe, so the server uses the best one the machine has.

14
00:01:25.717 --> 00:01:27.595
You don't need a GPU to start.

15
00:01:27.975 --> 00:01:32.732
Local models run on CPU, with llama.cpp, or through Ollama.

16
00:01:33.112 --> 00:01:42.179
So a laptop, or a server on a fully offline network, can run AI apps. The demo in this series ran a three-billion-parameter model this way.

17
00:01:42.559 --> 00:01:50.324
Context memory keeps each conversation's computed prompt, so follow-up questions on long contexts answer much faster.

18
00:01:50.704 --> 00:01:59.707
In our CPU benchmark, prefix-aware routing cut the warm time to first token from about one point two seconds to under a tenth of a second.

19
00:02:00.087 --> 00:02:04.503
On GPU servers, the same file runs models with vLLM.

20
00:02:05.583 --> 00:02:10.020
For GPU servers, the server runs and supervises vLLM.

21
00:02:10.400 --> 00:02:13.877
Here is what it sees on an emulated eight-GPU server.

22
00:02:14.257 --> 00:02:21.319
Each model gets its own GPUs, with tensor parallelism across them, and each engine sees only its own.

23
00:02:21.699 --> 00:02:27.757
If the GPUs can't hold every model, the change is refused, with a message saying what's free.

24
00:02:28.137 --> 00:02:35.476
This is preview: tested on emulated GPUs. Real GPU numbers are exactly what we need hardware for.

25
00:02:36.556 --> 00:02:41.612
Next: CXL memory, which adds capacity on memory-only NUMA nodes.

26
00:02:41.992 --> 00:02:53.555
The server keeps hot vector collections in DRAM, lets the kernel tier warm ones onto CXL, and moves unused collections to encrypted files on disk, loading them back on demand.

27
00:02:53.935 --> 00:02:55.385
Then data processors.

28
00:02:55.765 --> 00:03:02.976
The server detects BlueField cards, and runs encryption, checksums and TLS behind one data-plane driver.

29
00:03:03.356 --> 00:03:14.343
The BlueField driver itself isn't built yet. It needs a real card and NVIDIA's DOCA SDK. Until then, the host CPUs do this work, and the server says why.

30
00:03:15.423 --> 00:03:17.343
Finally: confidential apps.

31
00:03:17.723 --> 00:03:27.600
Mark an app confidential, and it runs only on servers that prove, with AMD SEV-SNP or Intel TDX attestation, what they're running on.

32
00:03:27.980 --> 00:03:30.519
A server that can't attest refuses to run it.

33
00:03:30.899 --> 00:03:37.896
With a key broker, the app's data key is released only to attested servers, so a copied data folder can't be read.

34
00:03:38.276 --> 00:03:43.161
Audit records carry the attestation, so an auditor can check where data was processed.

35
00:03:43.541 --> 00:03:47.488
This is tested on an emulated attestation platform so far.

36
00:03:48.568 --> 00:03:49.741
That's where you can help.

37
00:03:50.121 --> 00:04:00.489
To move these features out of preview, we need time on real hardware: a GPU server, a BlueField card, a CXL machine, or a confidential VM.

38
00:04:00.869 --> 00:04:10.704
If you have access, or cloud credits, open a hardware offer on the GitHub repository. A few days of remote access can verify a feature for everyone.

39
00:04:11.784 --> 00:04:15.368
Next in this series: time to market, with the ten built-in apps.

40
00:04:15.748 --> 00:04:22.703
AI App Server is free to use, and its SDKs, example apps and templates are Apache 2.0.

41
00:04:23.083 --> 00:04:29.611
Download it and follow the ten-minute quickstart at github.com/Sandaal/ai-app-server.

